<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A TAKE'S GAIN IS READ IN THE TAKE'S OWN DOMAIN (QBX-185).

  WHY THIS NEEDS ITS OWN CASE. `take_gain_per_region` gates that each take's
  volume, fade, pan and envelope are heard over that take's own regions, and
  it does so with BOTH takes at full extent — where a take's own domain and
  the column's are the same thing, because `tStart` is 0 for both. So nothing
  there can tell a build that converts the position into the take's domain from
  one that forgets to: the conversion is an identity in that fixture. Measured,
  not assumed: dropping the conversion leaves `take_gain_per_region` green.

  Here take 1 is PARTIAL — extent [96000, 192000), so its own frame 0 is the
  column's 96000 — and it carries a FADE, which is the one factor that depends
  on WHERE in the take you are.

  THE SHAPE. One 4 s column over test_sawtooth.wav (per-source-second RMS
  0.0667 / 0.1764 / 0.2906 / 0.4055):

    take 0 — FULL extent [0, 192000), source from 0, ACTIVE
    take 1 — PARTIAL [96000, 192000), source from 0

  Take 1 reads the source from its START while sitting in the column's second
  half, so over its 2 s it carries source seconds 0 and 1: 0.0667 then 0.1764.

  Map `96000 -> 1`, and take 1 gets a LINEAR fade-in of 48000 frames over its
  own 96000-frame window. In the take's own domain the ramp runs 0 to 1 across
  its first second and is DONE for its second:

    column [2 s, 3 s)  = take frames [0, 48000)      -> source second 0, ramped
    column [3 s, 4 s)  = take frames [48000, 96000)  -> source second 1, full

  The ramped window is the measurement: **0.0516268**, against 0.0666501
  unramped.

  AND NOT THE 0.0385 THE OBVIOUS ARITHMETIC PREDICTS, which is worth recording
  because the first draft of this case asserted it and failed. The estimate
  "0.0667 times the RMS of a 0-to-1 linear ramp, sqrt(1/3) = 0.5774" assumes
  the source is STATIONARY across the window. test_sawtooth is not: its
  amplitude ramps continuously (that is what makes its per-second RMS climb
  0.0667 / 0.1764 / 0.2906 / 0.4055), so within source second 0 the signal
  GROWS, and a fade-in weights the later, louder part more heavily. The result
  is higher than the stationary estimate. The fade-OUT below is the mirror
  image and lands LOWER than its estimate for the same reason: 0.0843199
  against a predicted 0.1019.

  So both numbers here are MEASURED. On a ramping source an RMS-times-ramp
  estimate is the right order of magnitude and the wrong value, and a bound
  set from it is a bound that fails on correct code.

  A BUILD THAT FORGETS THE CONVERSION evaluates the fade at the COLUMN
  position instead. At column 96000 that is already past a 48000-frame
  fade-in, so the gain reads 1.0 for the whole region and the first window
  reads 0.0666501 — the unramped value. That is the single number this case
  exists for, and the bound below excludes it deliberately.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="take_gain_take_domain">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- take 1: the column's SECOND HALF, reading the source from 0, and not
         active. `at`/`length` are what make its own domain differ from the
         column's, which is the whole point of this case. -->
    <add-take clip="0,0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" startOffset="0"
              index="-1" activate="0" at="96000" length="96000"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="0"
                        extents="0-192000;96000-192000" duration="192000"/>

    <set-comp-map clip="0,0">
      <seg at="96000" take="1"/>
    </set-comp-map>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" comp="96000:1" activeTake="0"/>

    <!-- THE BASELINE: take 1 at full gain over its own two source seconds. -->
    <render filename="tgtd_flat.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgtd_flat.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.055" maxRms="0.080"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgtd_flat.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>

    <!-- THE FADE, in the take's own domain. The ramp covers the take's first
         second, which is the column's THIRD. -->
    <set-clip-fade clip="0,0" take="1" fadeIn="48000" fadeOut="0"
                   shape="linear"/>
    <render filename="tgtd_faded.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <!-- THE MEASUREMENT. Correct: 0.0516268. A build reading the fade at the
         COLUMN position: 0.0666501, the unramped value, because column frame
         96000 is already past a 48000-frame fade-in. The bound holds the
         first and excludes the second. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgtd_faded.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.047" maxRms="0.057"/>
    <!-- ...and the take's SECOND second is past the ramp, so it is untouched.
         This is the other half of "in the take's domain": a build that scaled
         the whole region would fail here. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgtd_faded.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>
    <!-- take 0's half never had a fade and must not acquire one. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgtd_faded.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.055" maxRms="0.080"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgtd_faded.wav"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>

    <!-- A FADE-OUT IS ANCHORED TO THE END OF THE TAKE'S OWN WINDOW, which is
         what `fadeLen` carries and why it is the take's own length rather than
         its extent in the column's frames. 48000 frames out of a 96000-frame
         window ramps the take's SECOND second down to zero: the column's
         fourth second, whose 0.176362 becomes 0.0843199 (measured; see the
         header for why it is below the stationary estimate of 0.1019). -->
    <set-clip-fade clip="0,0" take="1" fadeIn="0" fadeOut="48000"
                   shape="linear"/>
    <render filename="tgtd_out.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgtd_out.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.055" maxRms="0.080"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgtd_out.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.078" maxRms="0.090"/>

    <!-- Clear it, and the baseline returns byte for byte. -->
    <set-clip-fade clip="0,0" take="1" fadeIn="0" fadeOut="0"/>
    <render filename="tgtd_restored.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="tgtd_restored.wav" expected="tgtd_flat.wav"/>

    <!-- AND THE VOLUME, which is position-INDEPENDENT and therefore says
         nothing about the domain — kept because it is the cheap half of the
         claim and because it pins that a PARTIAL take's trim reaches only its
         own regions, which is the shape mode A (P2) produces for every
         recording over existing material. -->
    <set-clip-volume clip="0,0" take="1" volumeDb="-20"/>
    <render filename="tgtd_quiet.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgtd_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.055" maxRms="0.080"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgtd_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.0055" maxRms="0.0080"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgtd_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.015" maxRms="0.021"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
